package com.bqsqwad.heartbreakhealer.feature.entertainment

/** Original ten-part mystery about a town clock, a school garden, and a misplaced set of records. */
internal object StoryLibraryBatch42 {
    val stories = listOf(
        StorySeries(
            id = "the_minutes_between_bells",
            title = "The Minutes Between Bells",
            tagline = "Two students investigate a clock that runs five minutes fast and uncover a forgotten school tradition.",
            parts = listOf(
                StoryPart(1, "Five Minutes Ahead", listOf(
                    "At Northbridge School, the bell rang at exactly noon, but the large clock above the assembly hall always showed twelve-oh-five. Students joked that the clock was eager for lunch. Teachers had learned to ignore it, and the caretaker had written 'fast by five minutes' on a maintenance note. When Sola joined the student history club, she noticed something unusual: the clock had been correct in a photograph taken during the school's opening ceremony. Her friend Malik thought the mechanism had simply worn out. Sola wondered why nobody had checked the old records.",
                    "They asked the caretaker, Mr Danjuma, whether they could inspect the public maintenance log. He agreed and brought out a folder containing repair dates, inspection notes, and a diagram of the hall. The clock had been adjusted several times, but each repair returned it to the same five-minute difference. One note said, 'Keep the old setting until the founders' record is found.' The note was signed with the initials E.B. and dated fifteen years earlier.",
                    "The students showed the note to their history teacher, who explained that E.B. might be Mrs Bello, a former deputy head who had helped organise the school's anniversary. She was now retired, but the school office had a public contact address. Their teacher approved a message asking whether she remembered the clock. Sola copied the exact wording into her notebook. The clock might be wrong, but the records suggested that somebody had once wanted it that way."
                )),
                StoryPart(2, "The Anniversary Photograph", listOf(
                    "The school archivist found the opening-ceremony photograph in a large album. The clock face showed eleven fifty-five while the ceremony programme listed noon. Sola checked the photograph date and found that the ceremony had begun five minutes later than the printed schedule. The clock had not been fast at all; the programme had been early. Malik suggested that the clock might have been adjusted after the ceremony to match the printed time, creating the difference that everyone now noticed.",
                    "The archivist found another photograph from the school's tenth anniversary. In it, students stood beside a display titled 'Five Minutes for Our Future'. The phrase appeared on a banner, but the photograph did not explain what it meant. A list of anniversary activities mentioned a time capsule, a garden project, and a student pledge. The clock had been part of the display, although nobody had written down why.",
                    "Sola and Malik searched the public school newsletter archive. They found a short article about a tradition in which each graduating class donated five minutes of service to the school community. The minutes were not literal time on a clock. They represented a small contribution: helping in the library, planting a tree, tutoring younger pupils, or repairing a shared space under supervision. The tradition had stopped after the anniversary because the committee's records were incomplete. The students began to suspect that the clock was a reminder, not a technical puzzle."
                )),
                StoryPart(3, "The Retired Deputy Head", listOf(
                    "Mrs Bello replied to the school's message and agreed to visit. She recognised the maintenance note immediately. The clock had been set five minutes ahead during the anniversary display to mark the five-minute service pledge. The committee planned to return it to the correct time after the event, but the display became popular and the school kept the setting as a reminder. Over the years, the explanation was lost as staff changed. Mrs Bello had written the note so the caretaker would know that the setting was intentional until the founders' record could be located.",
                    "She explained that the founders' record was a booklet containing the original pledge and a list of the first student volunteers. It had been placed in a time capsule during the anniversary. The capsule was not buried; it was stored in a locked display cabinet in the old assembly room. When the room was renovated, the cabinet was moved, and nobody could remember where the capsule went. Mrs Bello thought the archive committee might have transferred it to the district history room.",
                    "Malik asked why the school had not simply continued the tradition. Mrs Bello said the pledge had become a slogan without a clear process. Some students felt pressured to volunteer for tasks they had not chosen, while others did not know what counted as service. The committee paused it until a fairer system could be designed. Sola wrote that down. If they found the booklet, they would need to understand the tradition honestly, including why it had stopped."
                )),
                StoryPart(4, "The District History Room", listOf(
                    "The history teacher contacted the district archive and arranged a supervised visit. The archivist checked the school transfer list and found a cabinet labelled NORTHBRIDGE ANNIVERSARY MATERIALS. Inside were programmes, banners, a model of the school gate, and an empty display case. The time capsule was not there. A paper inventory listed the booklet as 'returned to school with garden committee papers'. That was the first clue pointing back to the campus.",
                    "At school, the students checked the garden committee folder with permission. It contained plans for a courtyard garden, seed receipts, and a schedule of student helpers. One page had a sketch of the old display cabinet with a note: 'Booklet placed behind the service map.' The cabinet had been moved to the library store during renovation. The students asked the librarian to help inspect the labelled boxes rather than searching through them alone.",
                    "They found the cabinet wrapped in a protective cover. Its back panel had a shallow document slot, but it was empty. A small paper tab remained, showing that something had once been stored there. The librarian checked the transfer record and discovered that the booklet had been removed for scanning several years earlier. The scan had been saved under the wrong project title. Sola and Malik now had a clear next step: find the digital archive entry and confirm the booklet's contents."
                )),
                StoryPart(5, "The Booklet in the Wrong Folder", listOf(
                    "The school technician helped the archivist search the digital records. The booklet appeared under 'Courtyard Planting — miscellaneous'. Its scan was faint but readable. The first page described the five-minute pledge as an invitation, not a requirement. Students could choose one small contribution that suited their interests and abilities. The second page listed the first volunteers, including a group that had designed raised garden beds so pupils who could not kneel comfortably could still help plant.",
                    "A later page explained why the tradition had paused. The committee had received complaints that some students were assigned tasks without asking, and that public lists of volunteers made a few pupils feel embarrassed. The booklet recommended private sign-ups, a range of activities, and adult supervision. It also said that no student should lose access to ordinary school activities for choosing not to volunteer. Sola was relieved to find the original purpose was more thoughtful than the slogan suggested.",
                    "The final page contained a note from the founding head teacher: 'A gift of time must remain a gift.' Malik said that sentence should be part of any new version. They brought the scan to their history teacher and asked whether the school might consider restarting the pledge with clear safeguards. The teacher said the idea was worth presenting, but the students would need input from staff, pupils, and the accessibility committee."
                )),
                StoryPart(6, "A Better Kind of Pledge", listOf(
                    "Sola and Malik prepared a short proposal for the student council. They suggested a voluntary service week with different kinds of contributions: reading with younger pupils, helping label library shelves, tending plants, creating large-print signs, or sharing a useful skill during a supervised club session. Students could work alone or in groups, and no public list would identify who had declined. The proposal included a simple way to request an alternative task or take part without being photographed.",
                    "The student council discussed the plan with teachers and the accessibility committee. One pupil suggested that some activities should be available during lunch so students with after-school responsibilities could participate. Another asked for quiet tasks for people who preferred not to speak in front of a group. The committee added both suggestions. The school also agreed that every task would have a named staff supervisor and a clear description before students signed up.",
                    "Mrs Bello reviewed the revised proposal and said it preserved the original idea better than the old slogan. The head teacher approved a small pilot rather than a permanent programme. The five-minute phrase would remain a symbol, but students could contribute more or less depending on the task and their circumstances. Nobody would be measured by a number. Sola thought the clock might finally have a purpose people understood."
                )),
                StoryPart(7, "The Clock Is Checked", listOf(
                    "Before the pilot began, Mr Danjuma inspected the clock mechanism with a qualified technician. They found that the clock had been adjusted to run five minutes fast and had continued that way for years. The technician explained that the mechanism was safe but needed a proper service. The school decided to restore it to the correct time and place a small plaque beneath it explaining the anniversary tradition. The old setting would be recorded in the archive rather than preserved as a confusing daily error.",
                    "The technician completed the service and set the clock accurately. For the first time in years, its hands matched the classroom clocks. Some students said the hall looked strange without the familiar five-minute difference. Sola understood. The old clock had become part of the school's routine, even though most people no longer knew why. The plaque would help preserve the memory without requiring the clock itself to remain inaccurate.",
                    "Malik suggested adding a small display with the original booklet scan and photographs of the first garden volunteers. The archivist approved a display using copies, not the fragile original. The school also added a note explaining that the service pledge was voluntary and that students could suggest new activities. The clock now measured time correctly, while the display explained why five minutes had once meant something more."
                )),
                StoryPart(8, "Five Minutes of Help", listOf(
                    "The pilot began on a Monday. A group of students helped organise the library's returned books, while another group prepared plant labels for the courtyard garden. Two pupils recorded a short audio guide for visitors, and a quiet team checked whether classroom signs were easy to read from different seats. Each activity had a supervisor and a clear finish time. Students could leave when their agreed task was complete, without being asked to explain personal circumstances.",
                    "Sola chose the garden team because she liked working outdoors. Malik joined the audio-guide group and discovered that recording a clear explanation took more planning than he expected. He wrote a script, checked every fact with the teacher, and recorded several versions before the team approved one. The audio guide included a transcript so visitors could choose how to use it. Nobody was required to appear in the recording.",
                    "At the end of the week, the student council collected anonymous feedback. Most comments praised the variety of tasks. A few students said they had not understood where to sign up, so the school improved the notice and added a simple paper option. One pupil suggested a future activity to help new students learn the library layout. The pilot had not solved every problem, but it had created a fairer way to offer help."
                )),
                StoryPart(9, "The Founders' Record", listOf(
                    "The school held a small assembly to display the scanned booklet and recognise the original volunteers. Mrs Bello attended with two former students who had helped build the raised garden beds. They described how the project had taught them to plan carefully, listen to one another, and make changes when someone could not use the original design. The current students asked questions about the early days, and the former volunteers explained that the best ideas had often come from pupils who noticed a practical problem.",
                    "The head teacher announced that the service week would continue twice each year, subject to review. Participation would remain voluntary, and the school would keep a range of tasks available. The archive would preserve the booklet and the reasons the earlier programme had paused. That history mattered because it showed that good intentions still needed thoughtful rules and honest feedback.",
                    "After the assembly, Sola and Malik placed a copy of the booklet in the display cabinet. The original returned to the archive for safe storage. Beneath the clock, the new plaque read: 'Five minutes for our future: a reminder that small contributions matter when they are freely given.' The students stepped back to read it. The clock ticked steadily above them, no longer carrying a confusing message."
                )),
                StoryPart(10, "The Minutes Between Bells", listOf(
                    "At the end of term, the school reviewed the second service week. The library had a clearer shelf system, the courtyard garden had new raised planters, and the audio guide helped visitors find the main rooms. The student council published a summary of the activities without ranking participants or naming anyone who had chosen not to join. Teachers agreed to keep the programme flexible and to ask for feedback before each new round.",
                    "Mrs Bello gave the school the original anniversary banner, which the archivist stored with the other records. She told Sola that the clock had never been intended to make anyone late. It was a visible reminder that a little time, offered freely and used thoughtfully, could improve a shared place. Sola thanked her for explaining the history and for being honest about the reasons the tradition had stopped.",
                    "On the final Friday, the bell rang at noon and the clock showed noon too. Malik checked it twice, then grinned. The five-minute difference had disappeared, but the idea behind it had returned in a better form. Sola walked past the courtyard, where a younger pupil was reading the new plant labels aloud to a friend. Between one bell and the next, the school had learned to give time without demanding it. That was the real measure the old clock had been waiting to keep."
                ))
            )
        )
    )
}
